# Local development environment variables. Copy to `.env` and fill in real
# values — `.env` is gitignored, never commit real secrets. See
# docs/environment-variables.md for the full reference (what each variable
# protects, which environments need it, how to generate a value) and
# docs/milestone-8-subphase-a-production-architecture-research.md Section 13
# for the original production-readiness audit that identified SCHEDULER_SECRET
# as undocumented.

# App tables live in the `store_spy` Postgres schema (control-plane split,
# migration 20260828120000); `control_plane.*` holds the shared identity layer.
# Do NOT add `?schema=public` — it pins search_path to `public` and breaks all
# raw SQL. Do NOT use `?schema=store_spy` — it breaks `prisma migrate`. The
# `options=-c search_path=store_spy,public` form is the one that works for both
# runtime and migrations. See docs/environment-variables.md.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/ecommerce_intel?options=-c%20search_path%3Dstore_spy%2Cpublic"

# Required. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# Unique per environment — never share between local/staging/production.
AUTH_SECRET=""

# REQUIRED in staging/production on any non-Vercel host (Render, Fly.io, etc).
# Auth.js only auto-trusts the request Host header when NODE_ENV !== "production"
# (true for local `next dev`) or when AUTH_URL/VERCEL/CF_PAGES is set — none of
# which apply to a `next start` deployment on Render. Without this, every
# Auth.js endpoint (/api/auth/session, /csrf, /providers, sign-in callbacks)
# fails closed with an UntrustedHost error — discovered by running a real
# `next start` locally in Milestone 8 Sub-phase C. Not needed locally (`next
# dev` already trusts the host). See https://errors.authjs.dev#untrustedhost.
AUTH_TRUST_HOST="true"

# Optional — omit to run without Google/Facebook sign-in (the Credentials
# email/password provider still works with none of these set).
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=""
FACEBOOK_CLIENT_ID=""
FACEBOOK_CLIENT_SECRET=""

# Optional locally — required for marketing/scheduler.ts and
# scripts/marketing-scheduler-tick.ts (Google advertising intelligence via
# SerpApi's Ads Transparency Center API, see src/lib/marketing/source-factory.ts).
# Without it, marketing collection is simply never triggered — every other
# feature works unaffected. Get a key at https://serpapi.com/manage-api-key.
SERPAPI_API_KEY=""

# Optional locally, REQUIRED in staging/production — authenticates calls to
# POST /api/internal/scheduler/tick and .../marketing-tick (the
# "x-scheduler-secret" request header). Both routes fail CLOSED (HTTP 503)
# if this is unset, so local `next dev` works fine without it as long as you
# only trigger crawls via `npm run scheduler:tick` / `npm run
# marketing-scheduler:tick` (which call the scheduler functions directly,
# not over HTTP) rather than the HTTP routes themselves.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
SCHEDULER_SECRET=""

# Optional locally, REQUIRED in staging/production — signs the HMAC token in
# every unsubscribe link (Milestone 12 §4.1, GET /unsubscribe). A dedicated
# secret, NOT AUTH_SECRET reused — a leaked unsubscribe link must never also
# compromise session signing. Fails closed the same way TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY
# does: unset means every unsubscribe link is rejected as invalid, never
# silently accepted unverified. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
UNSUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_SECRET=""

# Optional locally, REQUIRED in staging/production — signs the HMAC token in
# every email-confirmation link (GET /verify-email). A dedicated secret, NOT
# AUTH_SECRET or UNSUBSCRIBE_TOKEN_SECRET reused — same "a leaked link must
# never compromise anything else" reasoning. Bound to userId+email, not just
# userId, so a token stops verifying if the account's email ever changes.
# Fails closed: unset means every confirmation link is rejected as invalid.
# Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
EMAIL_VERIFICATION_TOKEN_SECRET=""

# Optional locally (verification/marketing email simply won't send —
# sendVerificationEmail()'s failure is caught and logged, never fatal to the
# request), REQUIRED in staging/production. Resend API key — see
# https://resend.com/api-keys. RESEND_API_KEY and EMAIL_FROM must BOTH be set
# or nothing sends (isEmailConfigured() in resend-client.ts).
RESEND_API_KEY=""

# The From address every outbound email (currently: email verification) is
# sent as. For local testing without a verified domain, Resend's sandbox
# sender "onboarding@resend.dev" works but can only deliver to the email
# address on your OWN Resend account. A real deployment needs a verified
# sending domain and a real From address on that domain.
EMAIL_FROM=""

# Optional — defaults to 1 (Render puts exactly one reverse proxy in front of
# the web service). How many x-forwarded-for entries, counted from the
# right, come from proxies you actually trust — see
# src/lib/security/rate-limit.ts's getClientIp(). 0 means "no proxy at all,
# ignore x-forwarded-for entirely." Only change this if the deployment
# topology changes (e.g. a CDN added in front of Render).
TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS="1"

# Optional — Milestone 12 §4.2 Step 2, the Meta (Facebook) base Pixel.
# BOTH the flag and the ID must be set for the pixel to actually load — see
# src/lib/marketing/pixels/meta.ts. Public identifier, not a secret: this
# is exactly as public as every real Meta Pixel already is (visible in the
# page source of any site using one). Client-side only, inlined at build
# time — never read on the server. Loads only on /, /terms, /privacy, and
# only after the visitor accepts the cookie consent banner.
NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ENABLED="false"
NEXT_PUBLIC_META_PIXEL_ID=""

# Optional — the server-side half of the same vendor (Meta Conversions
# API), dispatched from the worker for the one conversion event this app
# currently tracks (signup). This IS a real secret, unlike the pixel ID
# above — but Milestone 12 §4.3 (the credential vault) hasn't shipped yet,
# so there is nowhere safe to actually configure or rotate it this
# milestone. Setting it locally will make dispatchMetaConversionEvent()
# throw (a documented, deliberate PROVIDER SEAM — see that function's own
# comment) rather than silently succeed; leave it unset until §4.3 exists.
META_CONVERSIONS_API_ACCESS_TOKEN=""

# Optional — Milestone 12 §4.2 Step 2, vendor 2: Google Ads + GA4's client
# base tag (page_view/session telemetry only, GA4 measurement ID only —
# deliberately never a Google Ads AW- id, see src/lib/marketing/pixels/
# google.ts). BOTH the flag and the ID must be set for the tag to actually
# load. Public identifier, not a secret. Client-side only, inlined at build
# time. Loads only on /, /terms, /privacy, and only after cookie consent.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA4_MEASUREMENT_ENABLED="false"
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID=""

# Optional — the server-side half of the same vendor (GA4 Measurement
# Protocol), dispatched from the worker for the signup conversion event.
# This IS a real secret, unlike the measurement ID above — but Milestone 12
# §4.3 (the credential vault) hasn't shipped yet, so there is nowhere safe
# to actually configure or rotate it this milestone. Setting it locally
# will make dispatchGoogleConversionEvent() throw (a documented, deliberate
# PROVIDER SEAM) rather than silently succeed; leave it unset until §4.3
# exists. Reuses NEXT_PUBLIC_GA4_MEASUREMENT_ID above as the property to
# target — Measurement Protocol hits must target the same GA4 property as
# the client tag to unify in reporting, and that ID was never a secret.
GOOGLE_MEASUREMENT_PROTOCOL_API_SECRET=""

# Optional — Milestone 12 §4.2 Step 2, vendor 3: TikTok's client base pixel
# (page view only). BOTH the flag and the ID must be set for the pixel to
# actually load. Public identifier, not a secret. Client-side only, inlined
# at build time. Loads only on /, /terms, /privacy, and only after cookie
# consent.
NEXT_PUBLIC_TIKTOK_PIXEL_ENABLED="false"
NEXT_PUBLIC_TIKTOK_PIXEL_ID=""

# Optional — the server-side half of the same vendor (TikTok Events API),
# dispatched from the worker for the signup conversion event. This IS a
# real secret, unlike the pixel ID above — but Milestone 12 §4.3 (the
# credential vault) hasn't shipped yet, so there is nowhere safe to
# actually configure or rotate it this milestone. Setting it locally will
# make dispatchTikTokConversionEvent() throw (a documented, deliberate
# PROVIDER SEAM) rather than silently succeed; leave it unset until §4.3
# exists. Reuses NEXT_PUBLIC_TIKTOK_PIXEL_ID above as the pixel_code to
# report against — that ID was never a secret.
TIKTOK_EVENTS_API_ACCESS_TOKEN=""

# Optional — Milestone 12 §4.2 Step 2, vendor 4: LinkedIn's client base
# Insight Tag (page view only). BOTH the flag and the partner ID must be
# set for the tag to actually load. Public identifier, not a secret.
# Client-side only, inlined at build time. Loads only on /, /terms,
# /privacy, and only after cookie consent.
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINKEDIN_PIXEL_ENABLED="false"
NEXT_PUBLIC_LINKEDIN_PARTNER_ID=""

# Optional — the server-side half of the same vendor (LinkedIn Conversions
# API), dispatched from the worker for the signup conversion event. This
# IS a real secret, unlike the partner ID above — but Milestone 12 §4.3
# (the credential vault) hasn't shipped yet, so there is nowhere safe to
# actually configure or rotate it this milestone. Setting it locally will
# make dispatchLinkedInConversionEvent() throw (a documented, deliberate
# PROVIDER SEAM) rather than silently succeed; leave it unset until §4.3
# exists. Reuses NEXT_PUBLIC_LINKEDIN_PARTNER_ID above — that ID was never
# a secret.
LINKEDIN_CONVERSIONS_API_ACCESS_TOKEN=""

# Optional — Milestone 12 §4.2 Step 2, vendor 5: X (formerly Twitter)'s
# Conversions API, dispatched from the worker for the signup conversion
# event. UNLIKE every other vendor so far, X currently has NO client-side
# pixel — the operator asked to confirm the pixel adds anything beyond
# what X's own Conversions API + GA4's client tag already cover before
# building one; see src/lib/marketing/pixels/x.ts's own file comment. Both
# vars below are deliberately NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_`-prefixed (unlike every
# other vendor's identifier) since neither is read by client code yet.
# X_CONVERSIONS_API_ACCESS_TOKEN IS a real secret — Milestone 12 §4.3 (the
# credential vault) hasn't shipped, so setting it locally will make
# dispatchXConversionEvent() throw (a documented, deliberate PROVIDER
# SEAM) rather than silently succeed; leave it unset until §4.3 exists.
X_PIXEL_ID=""
X_CONVERSIONS_API_ACCESS_TOKEN=""
